Definition:
Design Patterns are proven, reusable solutions to commonly occurring software design problems.
They are not code, but templates/guidelines for solving problems in a clean, maintainable way.

Why Design Patterns? (Interview Favorite)
Improves code maintainability
Promotes loose coupling
Makes code scalable & testable
Widely used in Spring Boot & Microservices
Helps in clean architecture

Classification of Design Patterns:
| Category       | Purpose                  |
| -------------- | ------------------------ |
| **Creational** | Object creation          |
| **Structural** | Class/object composition |
| **Behavioral** | Object interaction       |

1️⃣ Creational Design Patterns
Definition:
Creational patterns deal with object creation mechanisms, trying to create objects in a manner suitable to the situation.

1. Singleton Pattern
Definition:

Ensures only one instance of a class exists and provides a global access point.

Key Points:
Private constructor
Static instance
Thread safety is important

Real-Time Usage:
Logging
Configuration
Spring Beans (default scope)

Purpose:
Ensure only one instance of a class exists.

Use Cases:
Configuration
Logger
Cache
Thread pool
Spring Beans (by default)

Example (Thread-Safe – Best Practice)
public class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {}

    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}

Output:
Singleton s1 = Singleton.getInstance();
Singleton s2 = Singleton.getInstance();

System.out.println(s1 == s2);
true

Interview Tip:
Spring beans are singleton scoped by default, but Spring Singleton ≠ JVM Singleton

2. Factory Pattern
Definition:

Defines an interface for creating objects but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate.

Key Points:
Hides object creation logic
Promotes loose coupling

Spring Usage:
BeanFactory
ApplicationContext

Example:
interface Notification {
    void notifyUser();
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void notifyUser() {
        System.out.println("Email sent");
    }
}

class SMSNotification implements Notification {
    public void notifyUser() {
        System.out.println("SMS sent");
    }
}

Factory
class NotificationFactory {
    public static Notification create(String type) {
        if (type.equals("EMAIL")) return new EmailNotification();
        if (type.equals("SMS")) return new SMSNotification();
        return null;
    }
}

Output:
Notification n = NotificationFactory.create("EMAIL");
n.notifyUser();

Email sent

Spring Boot Usage:
BeanFactory
ApplicationContext

3. Abstract Factory Pattern
 Abstract Factory Pattern
Definition:

Provides an interface for creating families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes.

Key Difference from Factory:
Factory → creates one object
Abstract Factory → creates related objects
Purpose:
Create objects without exposing creation logic

Purpose:
Factory of factories (create related objects)

Example:
interface Button {
    void paint();
}

class WindowsButton implements Button {
    public void paint() {
        System.out.println("Windows Button");
    }
}

class MacButton implements Button {
    public void paint() {
        System.out.println("Mac Button");
    }
}

interface GUIFactory {
    Button createButton();
}

class WindowsFactory implements GUIFactory {
    public Button createButton() {
        return new WindowsButton();
    }
}

Output:
Windows Button

Interview Line:
Factory creates one object, Abstract Factory creates families of objects

4. Builder Pattern
Purpose:
Create complex objects step-by-step

Example:
class User {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    private User(UserBuilder builder) {
        this.name = builder.name;
        this.age = builder.age;
    }

    static class UserBuilder {
        private String name;
        private int age;

        UserBuilder name(String name) {
            this.name = name;
            return this;
        }

        UserBuilder age(int age) {
            this.age = age;
            return this;
        }

        User build() {
            return new User(this);
        }
    }
}

Output:
User user = new User.UserBuilder()
                .name("Sai")
                .age(28)
                .build();

Real Usage:
StringBuilder
Lombok @Builder

2️⃣ Structural Design Patterns
5. Adapter Pattern
Purpose:
Convert one interface into another

Example:
interface PaymentGateway {
    void pay(int amount);
}

class OldPaymentSystem {
    void makePayment(int amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid " + amount);
    }
}

class PaymentAdapter implements PaymentGateway {
    private OldPaymentSystem oldSystem = new OldPaymentSystem();

    public void pay(int amount) {
        oldSystem.makePayment(amount);
    }
}

Output:
Paid 500

Spring Usage:
Controller → Service → External API adapters

6. Decorator Pattern
Purpose:
Add behavior dynamically

Example:
interface Coffee {
    double cost();
}

class SimpleCoffee implements Coffee {
    public double cost() {
        return 50;
    }
}

class MilkDecorator implements Coffee {
    private Coffee coffee;

    MilkDecorator(Coffee coffee) {
        this.coffee = coffee;
    }

    public double cost() {
        return coffee.cost() + 20;
    }
}

Output:
Coffee coffee = new MilkDecorator(new SimpleCoffee());
System.out.println(coffee.cost());

70

Spring Usage:
Filters
Interceptors

7. Proxy Pattern
Purpose:
Control access to object

Example:
interface Service {
    void execute();
}

class RealService implements Service {
    public void execute() {
        System.out.println("Executing service");
    }
}

class ServiceProxy implements Service {
    private RealService service;

    public void execute() {
        if (service == null) {
            service = new RealService();
        }
        System.out.println("Logging...");
        service.execute();
    }
}

Output:
Logging...
Executing service

Spring Usage:
AOP
Transaction Management
Security

3️⃣ Behavioral Design Patterns
8. Strategy Pattern (VERY IMPORTANT)
Purpose:
Encapsulate interchangeable algorithms

Example:
interface PaymentStrategy {
    void pay(int amount);
}

class CreditCardPayment implements PaymentStrategy {
    public void pay(int amount) {
        System.out.println("Paid using Credit Card: " + amount);
    }
}

class PaymentContext {
    private PaymentStrategy strategy;

    PaymentContext(PaymentStrategy strategy) {
        this.strategy = strategy;
    }

    void execute(int amount) {
        strategy.pay(amount);
    }
}

Output:
Paid using Credit Card: 1000

Spring Usage:
Multiple implementations injected via interface

9. Observer Pattern
Purpose:
Notify multiple objects on state change

Example:
interface Observer {
    void update(String msg);
}

class User implements Observer {
    public void update(String msg) {
        System.out.println("Notification: " + msg);
    }
}

class Subject {
    List<Observer> observers = new ArrayList<>();

    void add(Observer o) {
        observers.add(o);
    }

    void notifyAll(String msg) {
        observers.forEach(o -> o.update(msg));
    }
}

Output:
Notification: Order Placed

Microservices Usage:
Event-driven architecture
Kafka consumers

10. Template Method Pattern
Purpose:
Define algorithm skeleton, let subclasses implement steps

  Example:
  abstract class PaymentProcess {
      abstract void validate();
      abstract void pay();
  
      final void process() {
          validate();
          pay();
      }
  }
  
  Output:
  Validating
  Paying

11. Command Pattern
Purpose:
  Encapsulate request as object
  
  Example:
  interface Command {
      void execute();
  }
  
  class LightOnCommand implements Command {
      public void execute() {
          System.out.println("Light ON");
      }
  }
  
  Output:
  Light ON

Design Patterns in Spring Boot (Important)


Q1: Which pattern is most used in Spring?
👉 Proxy, Factory, Strategy

Q2: Difference between Factory & Strategy?
Factory → object creation
Strategy → algorithm selection

Q3: Is Singleton anti-pattern?
👉 Not always. Thread safety & testing matter.

Q4: How Spring handles multiple implementations?
👉 Strategy Pattern using @Qualifier

Q5: Which pattern supports Open/Closed Principle?
👉 Strategy, Decorator

